---
/**
 * Before and Behind, the fifth "in the original" essay: Janus, the god
 * Greece never had, read through the Greek that surrounds him (χάος,
 * πρόσσω καὶ ὀπίσσω, στροφή), the nymph Cranaë who tried to lose him, and
 * the hinge he paid her with (Ovid, Fasti 1 and 6). One reading column on
 * a dark sill lit from two sides: dusk blue for the face that looks behind
 * and for every Greek word, dawn gold for the face that looks before and
 * for every Latin one. A doorway on a fixed canvas swings with each
 * section's data-p; eight figures in the column each give the reader one
 * thing to touch (the ball that settles into a face, the coin, the Greek
 * doorkeepers, the direction of the future, the nymph's play, the hinge,
 * the cardinal wheel, the gates of war), and a wall of modern references
 * and a source ledger close it. Every claim is tagged text, reading or
 * disputed.
 *
 * Ported from the owner's Claude artifact
 * (claude.ai/artifact/A9QaADVPQnED6gs3yRNBqf); the essay text and its look
 * are the design of record. Its stylesheet is scoped under body.limen so
 * Poem.astro's line and section globals cannot reach it, and its script
 * ships as written (is:inline). The site's Janus mark links here
 * (JANUS_ESSAY, src/config/site.ts).
 */
import Poem from '../../layouts/Poem.astro';
import SeriesMark from '../../components/scroll/SeriesMark.astro';
import Byline from '../../components/scroll/Byline.astro';
import ScrollFoot from '../../components/scroll/ScrollFoot.astro';
import MethodLink from '../../components/scroll/MethodLink.astro';
---

<Poem
  title="Before and Behind"
  description="A visualized essay by Michael Wilhelm: Janus, the god Greece never had, the nymph who tried to lose him, and the hinge he paid her with."
  year="2026"
  night="#0e0d11"
  accent="#e9b45c"
  genre="Essay"
  bodyClass="limen"
  preload={['/fonts/cormorant-garamond-latin-500-normal.woff2', '/fonts/cormorant-garamond-latin-400-normal.woff2']}
>
  <canvas id="sill" class="stage" aria-hidden="true" data-twin="A fixed canvas behind the essay holds an arched doorway, lit dusk blue from the left and dawn gold from the right. Its two leaves swing wider or shut with each section, and motes drift through it in both directions."></canvas>
  <div id="bar" aria-hidden="true"><i id="bar-i"></i></div>
  <aside id="rail" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="v" id="r-top">read</span>
    <span class="tube"><span class="fill" id="fill"></span></span>
    <span class="v" id="r-bot">to come</span>
    <span class="v" id="state">outside</span>
  </aside>

  <main class="page"><div class="wrap">

  <header class="hero" data-p='{"open":0.35,"east":0.85,"west":0.85,"veil":0.1}'>
    <p class="log">
      <span>God <b class="gk" lang="grc">Ἰανός</b></span>
      <span>Faculty <b class="gk" lang="grc">ἅμα πρόσσω καὶ ὀπίσσω</b></span>
      <span>Price <b class="la" lang="la">ius cardinis</b></span>
    </p>
    <div class="headfig" data-twin="A head with two bearded faces looking in opposite directions. The left face is lit dusk blue and marked ὀπίσσω, behind; the right is lit dawn gold and marked πρόσσω, before."><svg id="hero-head" viewBox="-1.62 -1.1 3.24 2.2" role="img" aria-label="A head with two bearded faces looking in opposite directions, the left face lit blue and marked opissō, behind, the right face lit gold and marked prossō, before"></svg></div>
    <h1><span class="e">Before</span> and<br /><em>Behind</em></h1>
    <p class="deck">Janus, the god Greece never had, the nymph who tried to lose him, and the hinge he paid her with.</p>
    <p class="log sig"><span>an essay · <Byline /></span><span><SeriesMark id="before-and-behind" /></span></p>
    <div class="legend">
      <span><span class="k">Colors.</span> Greek is set in dusk blue, Latin in dawn gold.</span>
      <span><span class="tag" data-k="text" style="margin-left:0">text</span> an ancient source says it, cited at the foot. <span class="tag" data-k="reading">reading</span> my interpretation. <span class="tag" data-k="disputed">disputed</span> scholars argue the point.</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <hr>

  <!-- I -->
  <section data-p='{"open":1,"east":0.55,"west":0.55,"veil":0.3}'>
    <div class="body" style="--ml:0">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="gk" lang="grc">ΧΑΟΣ</span><span class="tr">chaos</span><span class="gl">the gape, the first opening</span></p>
      <p class="lede">Ovid opens the Roman year by telling Janus that Greece has no god like him. Janus answers with a Greek word.</p>
      <p>The question comes at the start of the <em>Fasti</em>, on the first of January. <span class="la" lang="la">Quem tamen esse deum te dicam, Iane biformis? Nam tibi par nullum Graecia numen habet.</span> What god shall I call you, two-formed Janus, when Greece has no divinity to match you?<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> Every handbook since has repeated the line, and it holds. No Greek city built him a temple, and Homer and Hesiod never speak his name.</p>
      <p>Then the god speaks, and the first thing he says about himself is this: <span class="la" lang="la">me Chaos antiqui, nam sum res prisca, vocabant.</span> The ancients called me Chaos, for I am an old thing.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span></p>
      <p>Chaos is Hesiod's word. <span class="gr" lang="grc">ἤτοι μὲν πρώτιστα Χάος γένετ᾽</span>: first of all, Chaos came to be. In Greek the word carries none of our mess and noise. <span class="gr" lang="grc">χάος</span> grows from the same root as <span class="gr" lang="grc">χάσκω</span>, to gape, and <span class="gr" lang="grc">χάσμα</span>, a chasm. It names a gap standing open.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> The Roman lexicon of Festus makes the join out loud: from Chaos the Greeks say <span class="gr" lang="grc">χάσκειν</span> and we say <span class="la" lang="la">hiare</span>, to yawn, and from that, with the breath dropped, comes <span class="la" lang="la">Ianus</span>, the first of all things.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> Linguists now trace <span class="la" lang="la">ianus</span> to a root for going and passage, and leave Festus his pun.<span class="tag" data-k="disputed">disputed</span></p>
      <p>The pun still shows how Rome read its own god. Asked where Janus stands in Greek, the tradition points to line 116 of the <em>Theogony</em>. Before Earth, before Eros, something opens. <span class="vein">Rome gave that opening a face on each side and a key.</span><span class="tag" data-k="reading">reading</span></p>

      <figure class="fig" data-twin="Fig. I, From a ball to a face: a slider settles a plain circle into a head with two faces. Ovid's Janus says that while fire, air, water and earth were one heap he was globus et sine imagine moles, a ball, a mass without a likeness, and that when the four settled he came back into a face. The matching front and back are the one mark the old shape left on him. Fasti 1.105–114.">
        <figcaption class="figk"><b>Fig. I</b> · From a ball to a face</figcaption>
        <svg id="globus" viewBox="-1.25 -1.15 2.5 2.3" role="img" aria-label="A circle that settles into a head with two faces as the slider moves right"></svg>
        <label class="ctl" for="settle">Settle the elements
          <input type="range" id="settle" min="0" max="100" value="100">
          <span class="ends"><span>globus</span><span>Ianus</span></span>
        </label>
        <p class="eq" id="globus-eq">r(θ) = (1 − t)·R + t·f(θ) · t = 1.00</p>
        <p class="cap">Ovid's Janus on his own beginning: while fire, air, water and earth were one heap he was <span class="la" lang="la">globus et sine imagine moles</span>, a ball, a mass without a likeness. When the four settled into their places, <span class="la" lang="la">in faciem redii</span>, I came back into a face. The matching front and back are the one mark the old shape left on him. <em>Fasti</em> 1.105–114.</p>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- II -->
  <section data-p='{"open":0.55,"east":0.9,"west":0.4,"veil":0.35}'>
    <div class="body" style="--ml:6%">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="gk" lang="grc">ΠΕΡΡΑΙΒΙΑ</span><span class="tr">Perrhaibia</span><span class="gl">the Thessalian country he is said to have left</span></p>
      <p class="lede">The Greeks who wrote about him gave him a passport.</p>
      <p>Plutarch was a Greek and a priest at Delphi, and in his <em>Roman Questions</em> he asks why Janus is shown with two faces. His first answer: because he was by birth a Greek from Perrhaebia, under Olympus, who crossed to Italy, settled among the people there, and changed his language and his way of living.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> Two faces for two lives. One for the tongue he was born into, one for the tongue he learned.</p>
      <p>His second answer turns the faces outward. Janus changed the Italians, from wild living to farming and civic order, and the second face is theirs. In the <em>Life of Numa</em> Plutarch leaves it open whether he was a daimon or a king.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span></p>
      <p>Draco of Corcyra, quoted by Athenaeus, fills in the papers. Janus invented garlands, rafts and ships. He struck the first bronze coin, which is why cities in Greece, Italy and Sicily stamp a two-faced head on one side and a ship, a raft or a garland on the other. He married his sister Camese, sailed for Italy, and settled the hill that kept his name, the Janiculum.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span></p>
      <p>The coins bear some of it out. Tenedos, the island in sight of Troy, was striking a double head by about 500 BC: a woman facing one way, a bearded man the other, a double axe on the back.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> Rome's bronze <span class="la" lang="la">as</span>, with Janus on the front and a ship's prow behind, came nearly three centuries later. The ship, Ovid has Janus explain, belongs to Saturn, the exiled god who arrived by boat and whom Janus took in. Posterity stamped it in bronze to witness the guest.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span></p>

      <figure class="fig" data-twin="Fig. II, Heads or ships: a coin the reader can toss. The Roman bronze as of about 225 BC carries Janus in front and, behind him, the prow of the ship that brought Saturn up the Tiber; Roman boys tossed it and called capita aut navia, heads or ships (Fasti 1.229–240; Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.7.22). The silver of Tenedos, about 500 BC, carries a woman and a bearded man joined at the back of the skull, with a double axe on the reverse. Nobody in Greece called the pair Janus.">
        <figcaption class="figk"><b>Fig. II</b> · Heads or ships</figcaption>
        <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Choose a coin">
          <button class="btn" id="coin-rome" aria-pressed="true">Rome · c. 225 BC</button>
          <button class="btn" id="coin-ten" aria-pressed="false">Tenedos · c. 500 BC</button>
        </div>
        <div class="coinbox" id="coinbox"><div class="coin" id="coin"><div class="side obv" id="obv"></div><div class="side rev" id="rev"></div></div></div>
        <div class="row"><button class="btn go" id="toss">Toss it</button><p class="out" id="coin-out" aria-live="polite">capita · heads</p></div>
        <p class="cap" id="coin-cap"></p>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- III -->
  <section data-p='{"open":0.3,"swing":1,"east":0.6,"west":0.8,"veil":0.35}'>
    <div class="body" style="--ml:1%">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="gk" lang="grc">ΣΤΡΟΦΑΙΟΣ</span><span class="tr">strophaios</span><span class="gl">of the hinge; also, the twisty one</span></p>
      <p class="lede">Greece kept the door. It split the job three ways.</p>
      <p>At the pivot stood Hermes Strophaios, Hermes of the Hinge. Aristophanes puts him on stage asking for the post, and the slave Cario hears the other meaning in the word and tells him the house has no use for twists.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> In front of the door stood Apollo Thyraios, Apollo of the Door, whose altars the Greeks set before their houses as lord of going out and coming in. The Roman antiquarian Macrobius points to him as the Greek match.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> At the gate and where three roads met stood Hekate, with three faces to Janus's two.</p>
      <p>The Etruscans next door had a two-faced gate god of their own, Culsans.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> Greece carved double heads on herms and wine cups and coins, and never built the two faces a temple.</p>
      <p>More than eight centuries after Aristophanes, the account is settled in Greek. Proclus, head of Plato's school at Athens, writes one hymn to two gods: <span class="gr" lang="grc">χαῖρ᾽, Ἑκάτη προθύραιε … χαῖρ᾽, Ἴανε προπάτορ.</span> Hail, Hekate before the door. Hail, Janus, forefather.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> In the next breath he calls him Zeus. <span class="vein">A successor of Plato prays to the Roman door in Homer's meter.</span></p>

      <figure class="fig" data-twin="Fig. III, Who keeps a Greek door: a doorway with three stations the reader can light. At the hinge, Hermes Strophaios, whose name also means twisty (Aristophanes, Wealth 1153–1154). Before the door, Apollo Thyraios, with a pillar or an altar in front of the house (Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.9.6). At the gate and the meeting of three roads, Hekate Before-the-Door (Proclus, Hymn 6). Lit together they are Janus: the hinge, the door, the gate and the passage itself in one body with two faces. Rome needed one name where Greece used three.">
        <figcaption class="figk"><b>Fig. III</b> · Who keeps a Greek door</figcaption>
        <svg id="keepers" viewBox="0 0 360 190" role="img" aria-label="A doorway with three stations marked: the hinge, a pillar in front of the door, and a meeting of three roads">
          <line x1="8" y1="160" x2="352" y2="160" stroke="rgba(236,229,214,.35)"/>
          <g fill="none" stroke="rgba(236,229,214,.55)" stroke-width="1.2">
            <path d="M150 160V46h78v114"/><path d="M142 46h94v-9h-94z"/>
          </g>
          <g class="gz" id="z-hinge">
            <path d="M150 50l36 9v97l-36 4z" fill="rgba(147,166,220,.14)" stroke="#93a6dc" stroke-width="1.2"/>
            <circle cx="150" cy="52" r="5" fill="#93a6dc"/><circle cx="150" cy="158" r="5" fill="#93a6dc"/>
            <path d="M150 158c-14 10-28 12-42 12" fill="none" stroke="#93a6dc" stroke-dasharray="2 3"/>
            <text x="104" y="184" text-anchor="end" class="svgl" style="fill:#93a6dc">στροφεύς</text>
          </g>
          <g class="gz" id="z-before">
            <path d="M268 160l11-62 11 62z" fill="rgba(147,166,220,.14)" stroke="#93a6dc" stroke-width="1.2"/>
            <path d="M260 160h38" stroke="#93a6dc" stroke-width="2.4"/>
            <path d="M274 96c2-8 8-8 10 0" fill="none" stroke="#93a6dc"/>
            <text x="279" y="184" text-anchor="middle" class="svgl" style="fill:#93a6dc">πρὸ θυρῶν</text>
          </g>
          <g class="gz" id="z-cross">
            <circle cx="62" cy="92" r="40" fill="rgba(147,166,220,.08)" stroke="#93a6dc" stroke-dasharray="2 4"/>
            <path d="M62 92V56M62 92L32 116M62 92l30 24" fill="none" stroke="#93a6dc" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round" opacity=".55"/>
            <circle cx="62" cy="92" r="6.5" fill="#0e0d11" stroke="#93a6dc" stroke-width="1.4"/>
            <text x="62" y="150" text-anchor="middle" class="svgl" style="fill:#93a6dc">τρίοδος</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Choose a station">
          <button class="btn" data-z="hinge" aria-pressed="true">The hinge</button>
          <button class="btn" data-z="before" aria-pressed="false">Before the door</button>
          <button class="btn" data-z="cross" aria-pressed="false">Gate and crossroads</button>
          <button class="btn" data-z="all" aria-pressed="false">All of it</button>
        </div>
        <div class="panel" id="keeper-panel" aria-live="polite"></div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- IV -->
  <section id="sec-prosso" data-p='{"open":0.7,"east":1,"west":1,"veil":0.3}'>
    <div class="body" style="--ml:8%">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="gk" lang="grc">ΠΡΟΣΣΩ ΚΑΙ ΟΠΙΣΣΩ</span><span class="tr">prossō kai opissō</span><span class="gl">before and behind</span></p>
      <p class="lede">Homer has the faculty and no god to hang it on.</p>
      <p>Four times in the epics a man is praised, or damned, by one formula. Achilles says Agamemnon has no idea how to think <span class="gr" lang="grc">ἅμα πρόσσω καὶ ὀπίσσω</span>, before and behind at once. Menelaus wants old Priam at the oath because an old man looks before and behind. Polydamas, the one Trojan who reads the war correctly, gets the line whole: <span class="gr" lang="grc">ὃ γὰρ οἶος ὅρα πρόσσω καὶ ὀπίσσω</span>, for he alone saw before and behind. Halitherses on Ithaca gets it again.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> It is Homer's phrase for the counselor worth hearing. Calchas the seer gets the long form: he knew what is, what will be, and what was before.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span></p>
      <p>Now the turn inside the word. <span class="gr" lang="grc">ὀπίσσω</span> means behind you in space. In time it means hereafter.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> Homer's people say the future lies at the back, and many readers take the picture as drawn: the Greeks faced what had happened, because it can be seen, and walked backward into what had yet to arrive. Linguists argue over how hard to press the image.<span class="tag" data-k="disputed">disputed</span> The dictionary fact stands either way. In Homer's Greek, the word for what is at your back is the word for what is coming.</p>
      <p>Give that to a god with a face on the back of his head. <span class="vein">In Latin his second face guards his rear. In Greek it watches tomorrow.</span><span class="tag" data-k="reading">reading</span></p>

      <figure class="fig" data-twin="Fig. IV, Which way is the future: a walking figure on a line of time, shown three ways. In English he faces what is coming. In Homer he faces what has happened, πρόσσω, before the eyes, and what is coming arrives at his back, ὀπίσσω. Janus has a face for each, and the face on the back of his head is the one that watches tomorrow.">
        <figcaption class="figk"><b>Fig. IV</b> · Which way is the future</figcaption>
        <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Choose a way of facing time">
          <button class="btn" data-o="english" aria-pressed="false">English</button>
          <button class="btn" data-o="homer" aria-pressed="true">Homer</button>
          <button class="btn" data-o="janus" aria-pressed="false">Janus</button>
        </div>
        <div class="orient">
          <div class="zone west" id="z-west"><span class="w" id="zw-w"></span><span id="zw-t"></span></div>
          <svg id="walker" viewBox="0 0 84 130" role="img" aria-label="A walking figure"></svg>
          <div class="zone east" id="z-east"><span class="w" id="ze-w"></span><span id="ze-t"></span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="flow"><span>time carries you this way</span><i></i></p>
        <p class="out" id="orient-out" aria-live="polite"></p>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="still" data-p='{"open":0.5,"east":1,"west":1,"veil":0.05}'>
    <p class="big">Every door has two fronts.</p>
    <p class="ref"><span class="la" lang="la" style="font-size:1.3em">omnis habet geminas, hinc atque hinc, ianua frontes</span><br />Janus to the poet · <em>Fasti</em> 1.135</p>
  </div>

  <!-- V -->
  <section data-p='{"open":0.08,"east":0.3,"west":0.55,"veil":0.45}'>
    <div class="body" style="--ml:3%">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="gk" lang="grc">ΑΝΤΡΟΝ</span><span class="tr">antron</span><span class="gl">a cave</span></p>
      <p class="lede">He has one love story, and Ovid saves it for the first of June.</p>
      <p>By the Tiber stands the old grove of Alernus, where the pontiffs still carry offerings. A nymph was born there. Earlier generations, Ovid says, called her Cranaë.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> She ran the fields with javelins and stretched her knotted nets across the valleys, and although she carried no quiver, people took her for Apollo's sister. Many men asked. None got anywhere, because she had a method.</p>
      <p>Whenever a young man spoke love to her, she gave the same reply.</p>
      <div class="q">
        <p class="la" lang="la">Haec loca lucis habent nimis, et cum luce pudoris: si secreta magis ducis in antra, sequor.</p>
        <p class="en">This place has too much light, and with the light too much shame. Lead me to a more hidden cave and I follow.</p>
        <p class="ref">Fasti 6.115–116</p>
      </div>
      <p>He would go ahead, believing her. She would reach the brush, stop, and be gone, to be found by no means at all.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span></p>
      <p>Janus saw her and wanted her. Ovid allows him one line of courtship: he used soft words on the hard girl. She ran the play she always ran, sent him ahead to find the cave, and dropped behind. Then the poet turns and speaks to her himself.</p>
      <div class="q">
        <p class="la" lang="la">Stulta! videt Ianus quae post sua terga gerantur.</p>
        <p class="en">Foolish girl. Janus sees what is done behind his back.</p>
        <p class="ref">Fasti 6.123</p>
      </div>
      <p>He takes her under the rock where she is hiding. The Latin is blunt: he seizes her in his arms, has what he hoped for, and only then speaks. Modern commentators name the scene plainly as a rape, and the poem's own vocabulary agrees. She is quarry, and then she is paid.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> The wooing took one line. The rest was a hunt, and she lost it.</p>

      <figure class="fig" data-twin="Fig. V, Run her play: a path from the grove past a rock to the cave, walked twice. Led by a suitor, the nymph follows, stops at the brush, and is gone when he turns at the cave mouth: nullo est invenienda modo, to be found by no means. Led by Janus, the rear face watches her stop: latebras respicit ille tuas, he looks back on your hiding place. Fasti 6.105–126.">
        <figcaption class="figk"><b>Fig. V</b> · Run her play</figcaption>
        <svg id="play" viewBox="0 0 360 200" role="img" aria-label="A path from a grove past a rock to a cave. One figure leads, the nymph follows and hides at the rock.">
          <line x1="6" y1="170" x2="354" y2="170" stroke="rgba(236,229,214,.35)"/>
          <g fill="rgba(236,229,214,.04)" stroke="rgba(236,229,214,.5)">
            <path d="M20 170v-28m0 0c-9 0-13-20 0-44c13 24 9 44 0 44z"/>
            <path d="M38 170v-36m0 0c-10 0-14-24 0-50c14 26 10 50 0 50z"/>
            <path d="M56 170v-26m0 0c-8 0-12-18 0-40c12 22 8 40 0 40z"/>
          </g>
          <text x="38" y="190" text-anchor="middle" class="svgl">lucus</text>
          <path d="M276 170c0-54 20-88 50-88s30 46 30 88z" fill="#15131a" stroke="rgba(236,229,214,.4)"/>
          <path d="M302 170v-26a15 15 0 0 1 30 0v26z" fill="#050408" stroke="rgba(236,229,214,.5)"/>
          <text x="317" y="190" text-anchor="middle" class="svgl">antrum</text>
          <polygon id="cone-f" fill="rgba(233,180,92,.17)"/>
          <polygon id="cone-b" fill="rgba(147,166,220,.22)"/>
          <path d="M160 170l5-27 16-11 20 6 9 17 3 15z" fill="#1c1921" stroke="rgba(236,229,214,.55)"/>
          <path d="M150 170c1-12 4-17 7-21M155 170c0-9 3-16 7-20M218 170c-1-10 1-15 4-18M224 170c0-7 2-11 4-13" fill="none" stroke="rgba(236,229,214,.4)"/>
          <text x="186" y="190" text-anchor="middle" class="svgl">rupes</text>
          <g id="nymph" stroke="#ece5d6" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round">
            <path d="M-5 0L0-15L5 0M0-15V-33"/><circle cy="-40" r="6.5" fill="#0e0d11"/><path d="M7-36a11 11 0 0 1 0 18"/>
          </g>
          <circle id="spot" r="19" fill="none" stroke="#93a6dc" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 4" opacity="0"/>
