  <!-- VI -->
  <section data-p='{"open":0.45,"swing":1,"east":0.85,"west":0.6,"veil":0.35}'>
    <div class="body" style="--ml:0">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="gk" lang="grc">ΣΤΡΟΦΗ</span><span class="tr">strophē</span><span class="gl">a turn, a dodge, the swing of a door</span></p>
      <p class="lede">What he says next is the reason the story exists.</p>
      <div class="q">
        <p class="la" lang="la">Ius pro concubitu nostro tibi cardinis esto: hoc pretium positae virginitatis habe.</p>
        <p class="en">For our lying together, let the right of the hinge be yours. Take this as the price of the maidenhood you have laid down.</p>
        <p class="ref">Fasti 6.127–128</p>
      </div>
      <p>Then he hands her a branch of whitethorn, with the power to drive harm from doorways. Ovid's nouns are legal and commercial: <span class="la" lang="la">ius</span>, a right; <span class="la" lang="la">pretium</span>, a price. Nobody asks her. A god sets the terms after the fact, and the calendar records the result. The first of June belongs to Carna, goddess of the hinge. <span class="la" lang="la">Numine clausa aperit, claudit aperta suo.</span> By her power she opens what is shut and shuts what is open.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span></p>

      <figure class="fig" data-twin="Fig. VI, The right of the hinge: a door the reader swings on its two pivots, with a sprig of whitethorn over the lintel. Opening it lights clausa aperit, she opens what is shut; closing it lights claudit aperta, she shuts what is open. Latin calls the pivot cardo, Greek στροφεύς. The whitethorn, spina alba, is her ward against whatever comes to a door at night.">
        <figcaption class="figk"><b>Fig. VI</b> · The right of the hinge</figcaption>
        <div class="doorway" id="doorway">
          <svg class="sprig" viewBox="0 0 150 40" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M6 30C40 14 96 12 144 26" fill="none" stroke="#7c8a5e" stroke-width="1.6"/>
            <path d="M30 22l-4-9M58 16l2-10M92 15l5-9M120 20l6-8M44 19l-6 8M76 14l-2 9M106 17l-3 9" stroke="#7c8a5e" stroke-width="1.1" fill="none"/>
            <defs><g id="blossom"><circle cx="0" cy="-3.2" r="2.4"/><circle cx="3" cy="-1" r="2.4"/><circle cx="1.9" cy="2.6" r="2.4"/><circle cx="-1.9" cy="2.6" r="2.4"/><circle cx="-3" cy="-1" r="2.4"/></g></defs>
            <use href="#blossom" x="26" y="11" fill="#f4efe2"/><use href="#blossom" x="60" y="5" fill="#f4efe2"/><use href="#blossom" x="98" y="5" fill="#f4efe2"/><use href="#blossom" x="127" y="11" fill="#f4efe2"/><use href="#blossom" x="76" y="25" fill="#f4efe2"/>
            <g fill="#e9b45c"><circle cx="26" cy="11" r="1"/><circle cx="60" cy="5" r="1"/><circle cx="98" cy="5" r="1"/><circle cx="127" cy="11" r="1"/><circle cx="76" cy="25" r="1"/></g>
          </svg>
          <div class="beyond"></div>
          <div class="leaf" id="leaf"></div>
          <span class="pin t"></span><span class="pin b"></span>
          <span class="pivot" lang="la">cardo</span>
        </div>
        <p class="hingeline" aria-live="polite"><span id="hl-open">clausa aperit</span><span id="hl-shut">claudit aperta</span></p>
        <label class="ctl" for="hinge">Turn the hinge
          <input type="range" id="hinge" min="0" max="100" value="36">
          <span class="ends"><span>shut</span><span>open</span></span>
        </label>
        <p class="cap">A Roman door hung on two pivots, top and bottom, each turning in a socket. Latin calls the pivot <span class="la" lang="la">cardo</span>, Greek <span class="gr" lang="grc">στροφεύς</span>. Over the lintel, the whitethorn: <span class="la" lang="la">spina alba</span>, her ward against whatever comes to a door at night.</p>
      </figure>

      <h3 class="run">The right changes hands.</h3>
      <p>Five books earlier, in January, Janus told the poet what he owned. <span class="la" lang="la">Me penes est unum vasti custodia mundi, et ius vertendi cardinis omne meum est.</span> The keeping of the wide world is mine alone, and the whole right of turning the hinge is mine.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> The phrase returns in June: <span class="la" lang="la">ius cardinis</span>. In January he holds all of it. In June he signs it over. He keeps the seeing and gives away the turning.<span class="tag" data-k="reading">reading</span></p>

      <h3 class="run">Her trick was already a hinge.</h3>
      <p>She was open, then shut. I follow; gone. Greek has one family of words for the move. <span class="gr" lang="grc">στροφή</span> is a turn, and it is a dodge, a wrestler's twist, a liar's swerve. <span class="gr" lang="grc">στροφεύς</span> is the socket a door pivots in. Hermes Strophaios is the hinge god and the twister in a single epithet.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> The nymph who lived by the <span class="gr" lang="grc">στροφή</span> is handed the <span class="gr" lang="grc">στροφεύς</span>. A dodge works until someone sees it. A hinge works every time, in plain sight, for whoever comes to the door.<span class="tag" data-k="reading">reading</span></p>

      <h3 class="run">Where she hid.</h3>
      <p>Behind. <span class="gr" lang="grc">ὀπίσσω</span>. The suitors walk toward what they want and lose it at their backs, which in Greek is where the future is kept. They face one way, so everything she does next is invisible to them. Janus is a door, and a door has two fronts. He cannot be sent ahead, because for him no ahead leaves a behind.<span class="tag" data-k="reading">reading</span> Her old name carries the ending inside it. <span class="gr" lang="grc">κραναή</span> is Homer's word for rocky Ithaca, and the rock is where she is found.<span class="tag" data-k="reading">reading</span></p>

      <h3 class="run">What she becomes.</h3>
      <p>She loses the open country, the nets, the long line of men left standing at cave mouths. She gains a post. When the <span class="la" lang="la">striges</span>, the screech-owls that drink from cradles, come through the window for the five-day-old Proca, she touches the doorposts three times with arbutus, pays the birds off with the entrails of a sow, and sets Janus's whitethorn in the window. The child lives to be a king of Alba.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> <span class="vein">The one who vanished at every threshold now decides what crosses one.</span></p>

      <h3 class="run">The seam.</h3>
      <p>Ovid has very likely joined two goddesses here. Carna, in the older accounts, looks after the heart and the liver and takes beans and bacon on her day. Cardea is the hinge, and the Church Fathers list her beside Forculus of the door-leaves and Limentinus of the sill.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> Many scholars think the nymph and her capture are the poet's own invention, made to weld the two names.<span class="tag" data-k="disputed">disputed</span> If so, the tale performs its subject. One name is turned on a pivot and comes out as another.</p>

      <h3 class="run">Everything else that turns.</h3>
      <p>Latin kept the word working. <span class="la" lang="la">Cardo</span> is the pivot of a door, then the pole the sky turns on, then the north–south line a surveyor draws across a new town, then the decisive moment: Virgil has Venus warn that Juno will not sit idle <span class="la" lang="la">tanto cardine rerum</span>, at so great a hinge of things.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> English keeps it in <em>cardinal</em>. Roman astrologers called the four angles of a birth chart the <span class="la" lang="la">cardines</span>.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> All of it is Carna's fee, still being collected.</p>

      <figure class="fig" data-twin="Fig. VII, What turns on the hinge: a circle with four hinge points around the word cardo, relabeled five ways. Compass: north, east, south, west. Year: Aries, Cancer, Libra and Capricorn, the signs where each season turns; January opens a few days after the lowest of them. Birth chart: Ascendant, Midheaven, Descendant and Imum Coeli, which Manilius and Firmicus call the cardines. Roman town: the cardo running north to south and the decumanus crossing it east to west. Virtues: prudence, justice, fortitude and temperance, named cardinal by Ambrose.">
        <figcaption class="figk"><b>Fig. VII</b> · What turns on the hinge</figcaption>
        <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Choose what the four hinges hold">
          <button class="btn" data-w="compass" aria-pressed="false">Compass</button>
          <button class="btn" data-w="year" aria-pressed="true">Year</button>
          <button class="btn" data-w="chart" aria-pressed="false">Birth chart</button>
          <button class="btn" data-w="town" aria-pressed="false">Roman town</button>
          <button class="btn" data-w="virtue" aria-pressed="false">Virtues</button>
        </div>
        <svg id="wheel" viewBox="-200 -150 400 300" role="img" aria-label="A circle with four hinge points and labels that change with the buttons above">
          <circle r="88" fill="none" stroke="rgba(236,229,214,.4)"/>
          <path d="M0-88V88M-88 0H88" stroke="rgba(236,229,214,.2)" stroke-dasharray="2 5"/>
          <g fill="#e9b45c"><circle cy="-88" r="5"/><circle cx="88" r="5"/><circle cy="88" r="5"/><circle cx="-88" r="5"/></g>
          <text class="c" y="6">cardo</text>
          <text id="w-n" y="-112"></text><text id="w-n2" y="-128"></text>
          <text id="w-s" y="122"></text><text id="w-s2" y="139"></text>
          <text id="w-e" x="146" y="-3"></text><text id="w-e2" x="146" y="15"></text>
          <text id="w-w" x="-146" y="-3"></text><text id="w-w2" x="-146" y="15"></text>
        </svg>
        <p class="cap" id="wheel-cap" aria-live="polite"></p>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- VII -->
  <section id="sec-gates" data-p='{"open":1,"east":0.7,"west":0.7,"veil":0.35}'>
    <div class="body" style="--ml:5%">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="gk" lang="grc">ΔΙΘΥΡΟΣ</span><span class="tr">dithyros</span><span class="gl">two-doored</span></p>
      <p class="lede">His public house in Rome was a passage with a door at each end, and the doors kept the city's count.</p>
      <p>Plutarch's Greek for it is <span class="gr" lang="grc">νεὼς δίθυρος</span>, a two-doored shrine, which they call the gate of war.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> Open, Rome was in arms. Shut, Rome was at peace. Livy says Numa built it as the index of the one and the other, and that after Numa it was shut twice: once when the first war with Carthage was over, and once by Augustus after Actium.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> Augustus had the arithmetic cut in bronze. Shut twice in all the years before me; three times under me.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span></p>
      <p>The poets disagree about what the doors hold in. Virgil chains Fury inside, on a heap of weapons, and bolts the gates on him.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> Ovid's Janus says the reverse: I stand open in war so the people who marched out can come home, and I bar the doors in peace so that Peace cannot leave.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> One passage, two fronts, and the meaning depends on the side you stand on.</p>

      <figure class="fig" data-twin="Fig. VIII, The gates, 753 BC to AD 14: a pair of gates the reader can shut, over a bar of 766 years drawn to one scale. The doors stand open for nearly all of it. They are shut for Numa's reign, forty-three years by Plutarch's count (715–673 BC by the traditional dates); briefly in 235 BC, after the first war with Carthage; in 29 BC, after Actium and Alexandria; in 25 BC, after the Cantabrian campaign; and a third time under Augustus, in a year the sources leave open.">
        <figcaption class="figk"><b>Fig. VIII</b> · The gates, 753 BC to AD 14</figcaption>
        <div class="gates open" id="gates"><div class="gate gate--l"></div><div class="gate gate--r"></div></div>
        <div class="row"><button class="btn go" id="gate-btn">Shut the gates</button><p class="out" id="gate-out" aria-live="polite">Open. Rome is in arms.</p></div>
        <div class="years">
          <div class="track" role="img" aria-label="A bar of 766 years, almost entirely open, with one band of 43 years shut near the start and three thin marks later">
            <span class="mk" style="left:4.96%;width:5.48%"></span>
            <span class="mk tick" style="left:67.62%"></span>
            <span class="mk tick" style="left:94.52%"></span>
            <span class="mk tick" style="left:95.3%"></span>
          </div>
