  </section>

  <!-- VIII -->
  <section data-p='{"open":0.6,"east":0.9,"west":0.5,"veil":0.45}'>
    <div class="body" style="--ml:0;max-width:none">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="gk" lang="grc">ΙΑΝΟΥΑΡΙΟΣ</span><span class="tr">Ianouarios</span><span class="gl">January, as Greek spells it</span></p>
      <p class="lede" style="max-width:30em">He was never retired. Two thousand years on, his name still goes on anything with two fronts.</p>
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Filter the references" id="filters">
        <button class="btn" data-f="all" aria-pressed="true">All</button>
        <button class="btn" data-f="words" aria-pressed="false">Words</button>
        <button class="btn" data-f="sky" aria-pressed="false">Sky and nature</button>
        <button class="btn" data-f="lab" aria-pressed="false">Lab</button>
        <button class="btn" data-f="mind" aria-pressed="false">Mind</button>
        <button class="btn" data-f="screen" aria-pressed="false">Screen</button>
        <button class="btn" data-f="machine" aria-pressed="false">Machines</button>
      </div>
      <div class="cards" id="cards">
        <article class="card" data-c="words"><p class="kk">Words · 153 BC</p><h3>January</h3><p><span class="la" lang="la">Ianuarius</span>, his month. Numa is said to have set it ahead of March, peace before war. From 153 BC the consuls took office on its first day, and the civil year has opened there since.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="words"><p class="kk">Words</p><h3>Janitor</h3><p>Latin <span class="la" lang="la">ianitor</span>, keeper of the <span class="la" lang="la">ianua</span>. Ovid's Janus takes the title at full rank: <span class="la" lang="la">caelestis ianitor aulae</span>, doorkeeper of the court of heaven.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="words"><p class="kk">Words · c. AD 430</p><h3>Heads or tails</h3><p>Roman boys flipping a coin called <span class="la" lang="la">capita aut navia</span>, heads or ships, after the two sides of the Janus <span class="la" lang="la">as</span>. Macrobius records the call long after the coin itself was gone.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="words"><p class="kk">Words · 1600s</p><h3>Janus-faced</h3><p>English for a person who shows one face and keeps another. The god's two faces meant full sight. The insult kept the anatomy and reversed the virtue.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="words"><p class="kk">Words</p><h3>Janus words</h3><p>Words that carry their own opposite: cleave, sanction, oversight, dust. Each one opens what it shuts.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="sky"><p class="kk">Sky · 1966</p><h3>Janus, moon of Saturn</h3><p>Found by Audouin Dollfus on 15 December 1966. It shares an orbit with Epimetheus, the Titan named Afterthought, fifty kilometers apart, and every four years the two trade places. It circles the planet named for the exile Janus took in.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="sky"><p class="kk">Nature</p><h3>Janus cats</h3><p>The name for cats born with two faces. One of them, Frank and Louie, lived fifteen years.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="lab"><p class="kk">Lab · 1989–91</p><h3>Janus kinase</h3><p>JAK first stood for just another kinase. It was renamed for its two near-identical domains set back to back, one that works and one that governs the first. JAK inhibitors now treat rheumatoid arthritis and eczema.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="lab"><p class="kk">Lab · 1991</p><h3>Janus particles</h3><p>Pierre-Gilles de Gennes, Nobel lecture: grains with one side that takes to water and one that refuses it. Packed along a surface they leave gaps, and in his phrase “the skin can breathe.”</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="mind"><p class="kk">Mind · 1971</p><h3>Janusian thinking</h3><p>The psychiatrist Albert Rothenberg's name for a move he kept finding in creative work: holding two opposed ideas as true in the same moment and building from both.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="mind"><p class="kk">Mind · 1978</p><h3>Koestler's <em>Janus</em></h3><p>Arthur Koestler's summing-up takes the god as the emblem of the holon. Every living unit shows one face to the parts it governs and another to the whole it serves.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="screen"><p class="kk">Screen · 1956</p><h3>Janus Films</h3><p>The American distributor that carried Bergman, Fellini and Kurosawa into art houses, under a two-faced medallion that still opens its prints.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="screen"><p class="kk">Screen · 1995</p><h3><em>GoldenEye</em></h3><p>The syndicate after the satellite is called Janus, and the man behind it was once agent 006.</p></article>
        <article class="card" data-c="machine"><p class="kk">Machines · 2024</p><h3>DeepSeek Janus</h3><p>A model with two visual pathways behind one mind: one for understanding pictures, one for making them.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- IX -->
  <section data-p='{"open":1,"east":1,"west":0.9,"veil":0.2}'>
    <div class="body" style="--ml:4%">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="gk" lang="grc">ΑΡΧΗ</span><span class="tr">archē</span><span class="gl">a beginning; also, command</span></p>
      <p class="lede">Rome put him first in every prayer, whichever god the prayer was for.</p>
      <p>Ovid asks why, and Janus answers like a porter: so that through me, who keep the thresholds, you can reach whichever gods you want.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span> On his Kalends people gave each other dates, figs and honey so the year would run sweet, and each did a little of his own trade so the year would be worked. Ovid asks about that too, and gets the line the whole calendar turns on. <span class="la" lang="la">Omina principiis inesse solent.</span> Omens tend to live in beginnings.<span class="tag" data-k="text">text</span></p>
      <p>Gather the Greek. <span class="gr" lang="grc">χάος</span> for what he is: the first opening. <span class="gr" lang="grc">πρόσσω καὶ ὀπίσσω</span> for what he does. <span class="gr" lang="grc">στροφή</span> for what he caught and what he paid. Greece had every word and never assembled the god. Rome set the words in one body, stood it in a doorway, and told one story about it: a woman who knew how to disappear behind a man, and the one figure who has no behind.</p>
      <p>The story is unkind to her, and Ovid lets it be. It is also exact. What you chase goes where you are not looking. Sight that takes in its own back ends the chase. <span class="vein">And the thing caught at the turn becomes the thing every door turns on.</span></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

